.SH NAME
ssoossh - The ssoossh client — turns an OIDC login into a short-lived SSH certificate, from your ssh_config.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBssoossh [command] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
The ssoossh client wires SSO into your existing SSH workflow. Configured as a ProxyCommand or Match exec in ssh_config, it generates a fresh keypair, hands the public key to the ssoossh server, opens your browser for OIDC authentication, and loads the signed certificate into your ssh-agent — or writes key and certificate files when no agent is available. Private keys never leave the machine. Valid certificates are reused until they expire, so authenticating once could cover a workday rather than every connection. Runs on macOS, Linux, and Windows, and also handles local principal mapping for AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand and service-account certificates for unattended jobs.


